# A block two branches decide, one on its dominator chain and one below
# it: the threads below 64 reach it where n != 0, or where n == 0 and
# m != 0, so its set is a bound, not the 64 threads of `%tid.x < 64`. On
# an RTX 4090 with n = m = 0 its barrier runs on no thread, within the
# bound. The same holds below a loop header (micro_or_entry_loop), whose
# latch the threads at or above 64 reach on every trip and the others
# where n or m decides. Where both branches read the thread index, the
# set is exact: of the threads below 64, those below 16 or from 48 store,
# 32 threads storing 512 B over the loop's 4 trips.
RUN: %ptxroof analyze %fixtures/micro/or_entry.ptx --ncu %fixtures/micro/or_entry.ncu.json | FileCheck "%s"
CHECK-LABEL: kernel micro_or_entry
CHECK: $L__BB0_3  (no line info)
CHECK-SAME: <= 64 in <= 2 warps (%tid.x < 64)
CHECK: $L__BB0_3  consistent
CHECK: line 40: bar.sync 1, 64;  predicted <= 64, measured 0
CHECK-LABEL: kernel micro_or_entry_loop
CHECK: $L__BB1_4  (no line info)
CHECK-SAME: <= 64 in <= 2 warps (%tid.x < 64)
CHECK: $L__BB1_5  (no line info)
CHECK-SAME: <= 128 in <= 4 warps ((%tid.x >= 64 or %tid.x < 64 and ?) or %tid.x < 64 and ? and ?)
CHECK-LABEL: kernel micro_or_entry_footprint
CHECK: $L__BB2_3  (no line info)
CHECK-SAME: 32 in 2 warps (%tid.x < 64 and (%tid.x < 16 or %tid.x >= 16 and %tid.x >= 48))
CHECK: global bytes per CTA over the loop's own blocks: requested 512 B
